{:activity/_scope [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120736} :activity/id #uuid "9bad4bff-0bb7-5ce9-89c2-cbdc3bbadd77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121058 :tutorial/content "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n![](images/b9/b91bd53b8f1807be516e2477b37fbcd9c3a7415d6cf848d84aa085bc510b5eb4.svg)\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6318f9cd-930b-5b7b-acc7-c27b5b241825" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3519 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "df182889-cfbe-5906-abbc-45fc241177c2" :step/math-academy-id 19977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119661 :question/id #uuid "7cce27e9-e2b2-599c-9f4e-437caf1a23ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-5415" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be3d5a74-259e-58b9-948d-47ba7412ab08" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547290 :question-generator/id #uuid "0ca56e1c-765c-56bb-9338-487d6907e8bc" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186119847.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253bc858-812c-5e74-983b-cd0de2eb0f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317682} {:answer/id #uuid "2a24cb2d-1445-54a0-bacf-08246573e89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317695} {:answer/id #uuid "334787ce-0a56-588a-a4c8-8b58eecb9d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317729} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8b9422-7271-5c25-b890-90a23aa7d66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317949} {:answer/id #uuid "be4b1522-823c-5a19-916d-dd2b3e04a7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318197}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317729} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0238a45-9484-588e-9353-4f4e202dc332" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5549e3a-28d3-5fc9-95bb-dda91eedbf45" :question/math-academy-id "q-34806" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $6\\times 11$ matrix that has $8$ **non-pivot** columns, then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ \n\nSince $A$ has $11$ columns and $8$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n11-8=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(A